From: Justin Pfister <119864532+JJRPF@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 22:38:16 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 19/37] tools/aurora-sep: idle-ab: log busy time per CPU; doc:
 E6 procedure

E6 boots the energy-model image and pairs EAS on against EAS off;
moving light work from the P clusters to the E cluster shows in each
CPU's busy time, which each window's log line now carries.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01AuzUDcEeRJQ6RCdWW2XWLb
---
 docs/deep-idle-research.md             | 14 ++++++++++++++
 tools/aurora-sep/idle-ab.py            | 17 ++++++++++-------
 tools/aurora-sep/tests/test_idle_ab.py | 20 +++++++++++++++++++-
 3 files changed, 43 insertions(+), 8 deletions(-)

diff --git a/docs/deep-idle-research.md b/docs/deep-idle-research.md
index 2dddc70f4295..3e292ee257e5 100644
--- a/docs/deep-idle-research.md
+++ b/docs/deep-idle-research.md
@@ -489,6 +489,20 @@ a few percent.
   then Wi-Fi's (`0000:01:00.0`), and measure with E2: the gain is tens of
   mW at most, invisible in an awake A/B. The pcie-apple driver disables link
   power states only for M3's MediaTek radios (`pcie-apple.c` around 2582).
+* **E6, energy-aware scheduling** (needs a reboot; owner's go-ahead).
+  Boot `boot.bin.em-test` the way E1 boots its image (same kernel; only the
+  DTB changes). Check that the energy model registered: `sudo ls
+  /sys/kernel/debug/energy_model` lists three performance domains (cpu0,
+  cpu4, cpu7), and `sudo cat
+  /sys/kernel/debug/energy_model/cpu0/ps:912000/power` reads 53000. Then,
+  with the machine as quiet as it gets (EAS stands aside while any CPU is
+  overutilized), `sudo tools/aurora-sep/idle-ab.py --rounds 20 --window 20
+  default no-eas` pairs EAS on against EAS off in the same boot, and
+  `pd-always` can join the same run. Expect light background work to move
+  from the P clusters to the E cluster (busy time per cluster in the log)
+  and Total System Power to drop when idle-ish; under load nothing changes.
+  Rollback is the same as E1's, or at runtime `echo 0 | sudo tee
+  /proc/sys/kernel/sched_energy_aware`.
 * **E3, does a raw-mode SoC wake reach m1n1?** This is the experiment that
   decides raw vs Mach-O for the wake path. It needs a second machine and USB
   m1n1 proxy, and is not for the daily driver without a plan. From the m1n1
diff --git a/tools/aurora-sep/idle-ab.py b/tools/aurora-sep/idle-ab.py
index 11dafa697c20..4747e22f604e 100755
--- a/tools/aurora-sep/idle-ab.py
+++ b/tools/aurora-sep/idle-ab.py
@@ -146,15 +146,14 @@ class Machine:
         return [[int((cpu / f'state{i}/time').read_text()) for i in (0, 1)] for cpu in self.cpus]
 
     def busy(self):
-        """(busy, total) jiffies over all CPUs: everything but idle and iowait."""
-        busy = total = 0
+        """{CPU number: (busy, total)} jiffies, busy being all but idle and iowait."""
+        out = {}
         for line in (self.root / STAT).read_text().splitlines():
             fields = line.split()
             if fields and fields[0].startswith('cpu') and fields[0][3:].isdigit():
                 ticks = [int(x) for x in fields[1:]]
-                busy += sum(ticks) - ticks[3] - ticks[4]
-                total += sum(ticks)
-        return busy, total
+                out[int(fields[0][3:])] = (sum(ticks) - ticks[3] - ticks[4], sum(ticks))
+        return out
 
     def mhz(self):
         """{policy name: current MHz}."""
@@ -184,7 +183,9 @@ def run(machine, names, rounds, window, settle, log=None):
                     clocks.append(machine.mhz())
                     time.sleep(1)
                 after, elapsed = machine.residency_us(), time.monotonic() - start
-                ticks = [b - a for a, b in zip(ticks, machine.busy())]
+                ticks = {cpu: [b - a for a, b in zip(ticks[cpu], now)]
+                         for cpu, now in machine.busy().items() if cpu in ticks}
+                busy, jiffies = (sum(t[i] for t in ticks.values()) for i in (0, 1))
                 spent = [[a - b for a, b in zip(*pair)] for pair in zip(after, before)]
                 total = elapsed * 1e6 * len(machine.cpus)
                 row = {'round': n, 'setting': name,
@@ -195,7 +196,9 @@ def run(machine, names, rounds, window, settle, log=None):
                        'pd_pct': 100 * sum(cpu[1] for cpu in spent) / total,
                        'pd_pct_by_cpu': {number: round(100 * cpu[1] / (elapsed * 1e6), 1)
                                          for number, cpu in zip(machine.numbers(), spent)},
-                       'busy_pct': 100 * ticks[0] / ticks[1] if ticks[1] else 0.0,
+                       'busy_pct': 100 * busy / jiffies if jiffies else 0.0,
+                       'busy_pct_by_cpu': {cpu: round(100 * b / t, 1) if t else 0.0
+                                           for cpu, (b, t) in ticks.items()},
                        'mhz': {name: statistics.mean(clock[name] for clock in clocks)
                                for name in clocks[0]}}
                 rows.append(row)
